\title{Eager reduction on the machine}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\meta{status}{implemented}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/review/eager-reduction.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented behind the switch \code{eager}, published in the main tree on 2026-11-24 and off by default; proposed for the default switch set. Without the switch the machine declines every declaration that enters eager reduction, see [the decision](dec-X2UF).}

\p{\strong{What the official kernel does.} When check-mode inference meets an argument of the form \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~}, the comparison of that argument's type with its domain runs with a flag set. The flag is read in exactly two places of the comparison: Nat folding is tried although a side has a free variable, and the \code{Bool.true} test reduces a side that has a free variable. Comparisons under the flag do not nest. Reduction and inference depend on the mode only through the K rule, the one place where the \em{answer} of a comparison changes the \em{result} of a reduction. The kernel's caches belong to one declaration, are keyed by terms whose free variables are unique per binder instance, and are read and written in both modes. So the official answer to a query is not a function of the query: it depends on the mode and on what the declaration computed before, in which mode, under which binder instance.}

\p{\strong{Why the machine's tables could not reproduce it.} The machine computes on hash-consed values with canonical free variables and keeps its memos for a session. A pair found convertible in plain mode was read under the flag, where the official kernel answers "true" only if \em{its} cache holds the pair: in the same binder instance yes, under another binder or in another declaration no. The same holds for memo words that went through the K rule. This was found three times: by a memo audit on an arena test (one pair entry, written in unit 112 of the file and read under the flag in unit 128; the verdict was the same in the end, so every differential gate was clean), and by the soundness review as wrong accepts: two declarations in a warm session (finding S-F4) and one declaration with two binder scopes in the plain machine (finding S-F5).}

\p{\strong{The mechanism of the switch.} It does not try to know which entries the official cache holds; it checks that it does not matter.}
\ol{
\li{\strong{Deferral.} The comparison of an \code{eagerReduce} argument is not run where it stands. The two types and the open binders are recorded, the plain phase goes on as if the answer were "true", and when every plain judgment of the declaration has completed the binders are re-opened and the comparison runs under the flag. A "false" is the official error of the application. So no plain judgment ever reads an answer derived under the flag.}
\li{\strong{Recorded points.} The plain phase of such a declaration runs \em{aware}: at each of the two places where the modes would branch differently it probes what the eager branch would have done, and records the pair if that branch would have answered. At the end the plain answer and the eager answer of every recorded pair are compared. \strong{A difference declines the declaration}: the official answer there would depend on the official cache. These are exactly the hostile pairs of S-F4 and S-F5.}
\li{\strong{The K rule.} An eager-only event inside the comparison of the K rule declines the declaration: the result of a reduction would depend on the mode.}
\li{\strong{Sessions.} A cheap, over-approximate session flag says whether anything the session holds could differ between the modes. A declaration that reaches its first \code{eagerReduce} argument with the flag set is run again in a fresh session, aware from the start; after a declaration that recorded a point or saw an eager-only event the session is forgotten.}
}

\p{\strong{What the argument assumes.} That the official procedure on terms and the machine's on values ask the same queries up to instantiation, beta and zeta. One gap is argued and not demonstrated: a K reduction whose plain and eager computation the machine both skips because its two operands are one value. No export for it could be produced, and it cannot be excluded; only an engine on terms is free of it. The mechanism also declines some declarations the official kernel decides: a recorded pair with two answers that is never asked under the flag, the K rule firing although its comparison has one answer, more than 4,096 recorded points. Resource differences can only become a decline, never a verdict.}

\p{\strong{Gates} (switch set \code{\verb~|default,eager~}, the published sources). Per declaration against the reference engine: the tutorial tests, the bugs tests, the Cedar slice (8,418 units), reachability, Init (58,547), all 230 arena tests: no difference and no decline; Std 101,145 of 101,146 and 16 Mathlib slices 436,404 of 436,408, the five missing units being declined by a rule of the typed-binding fix, not by eager reduction. The hostile suite under 33 switch sets, each with \code{eager}, 13,827 judgments against official verdicts: the only flagged rows are the three of the open finding E1-F7, which is not about eager reduction. 18 hostile rows are declined by design. Mutants, 17,775 for each of three switch sets: no soundness and no completeness difference. The adversarial review of the candidate default attacked the deferral (a comparison run with its binders closed, only the last of several run) with seeded faults; both are caught by the suite, the second only after a row was added, and the review found no false accept.}

\p{\strong{Declines before and after} (launcher with the machine as engine, 8 workers; prototype tree):}
\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Units declined, switch off}\th{Switch on}\th{Runs repeated in a fresh session}\th{Machine ticks, off to on}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{164}\td{0}\td{132}\td{39.47 M to 40.44 M (+2.5 \%)}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{203}\td{0}\td{168}\td{64.77 M to 67.24 M (+3.8 \%)}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{423}\td{0}\td{275}\td{84.74 M to 88.22 M (+4.1 \%)}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{8}\td{0}\td{5}\td{9.16 M to 9.15 M}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{6,711}\td{0}\td{1,999}\td{1,249.2 M to 1,303.8 M (+4.4 \%)}}
}

\p{On these corpora no recorded point has two answers and no eager-only event falls into a K comparison. The formerly declined units are cheaper on the machine than on the reference engine where they are heavy: the Std unit that bounds the 8-worker wall clock of Std takes 1,100 ms on the reference engine and 154 ms on the machine, both runs of the repeated check included (in process, loaded host: orientation only).}

\p{\strong{Warm wall clock} after the publication, 8 workers, check-only passes, quiet windows, best and median of the later passes, without the switch to with it. Native image: Std 1.16 / 1.19 s to 1.02 / 1.03 s and 1.17 / 1.22 to 1.03 / 1.04; cedar 1.56 / 1.59 to 1.40 / 1.42 and 1.56 / 1.58 to 1.39 / 1.42; Mathlib 23.92 s to 21.07 s (one row, 3 passes). JVM launcher: Std 1.00 / 1.16 to 0.82 / 0.94 and 0.97 / 1.02 to 0.89 / 0.99; cedar 1.33 / 1.65 to 1.15 / 1.18 and 1.25 / 1.28 to 1.16 / 1.19; Mathlib 20.07 s to 19.53 s. The prototype's first window on the JVM launcher had given -11 \% and -22 \% on Std and -7 \% and -10 \% on cedar. Not measured: one worker, and passes that reload the file.}

\p{\strong{Measured and rejected.} Running every declaration aware from its start removes most repeated runs but makes the probes run everywhere: machine ticks grow by a factor of 2.7 to 4.6 and new declines appear.}

